Reduce rectangular constraints with reversible row operations (@math-row-equivalence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Count independent constraints and describe every lost direction.lingua#r_009D1DD1D9B0EBCA56B64A22F0") { r_0040C3CC99E7DD621AA0C570AA
Continue [Solve simultaneous constraints without losing their solution set](Solve-simultaneous-constraints-without-losing-their-solution-set.lingua#r_03FC1F97250326F9B36A1B2F6B) with m equations and n real unknowns. Write [A|b] so every operation includes its right-hand side. Exchange two distinct rows, scale one row by a nonzero real number, or add c times another row to it. The inverse operations are the same exchange, scaling by 1/c, and adding −c times the unchanged other row. Therefore each step preserves exactly the solution set. Multiplying a row by zero erases information and is excluded.

Here is a finite elimination procedure. Start at row 1 and scan coefficient columns from left to right. In a column, search the current row and rows below it for a nonzero entry. If none exists, pass to the next column. Otherwise exchange such a row into the current position, divide by that entry to make a pivot 1, and subtract the required multiple from every other row to clear that pivot column. Advance one row and continue. There are at most min(m,n) pivots and n scanned columns, so the procedure terminates. Earlier pivot columns remain cleared because the new pivot row has zeros there.

The resulting coefficient matrix is in reduced row echelon form: zero coefficient rows are last, leading 1s advance strictly rightward, and each pivot column has zeros elsewhere. Ordinary echelon form requires zeros below pivots but need not clear above or normalize pivots. Rectangular or dependent systems can skip columns. There is no general requirement of a 1 in every diagonal position. The algorithm here pivots only in A; a zero coefficient row with a nonzero right-hand entry is then exposed as a contradiction.

For A with rows (1,0,1), (0,1,1), (1,1,2), (2,−1,1), and b=(2,1,4,2), replace row 3 by row 3 minus rows 1 and 2, and row 4 by row 4 minus twice row 1 plus row 2. The last two rows become [0,0,0|1] and [0,0,0|−1]. No x satisfies the exact problem. Replacing b by (2,1,3,3) makes both right-hand entries zero. Then x₃=t is free and the solutions are (2−t,1−t,t). These are complete descriptions, not merely examples.

In any reduced consistent system, assign arbitrary real values to the nonpivot variables. Each pivot equation then determines its own pivot variable, because its pivot is 1 and every other pivot column is zero in that row. Zero rows impose nothing further. This proves both existence for every free-variable assignment and exhaustion of all solutions. A contradiction row proves nonexistence before any attempt to choose free variables.

**Work:** perform each operation on the complete displayed arrays; substitute two members of the consistent family into the original equations. Construct a 2×3 matrix whose first column is zero but whose rank is 2, and solve a consistent system with it. Explain what goes wrong if pivot search is restricted to diagonal entries. Over nonnegative or integer unknowns, intersect the real solution family with that extra domain; real elimination alone does not answer the restricted problem. With t integer and all three components nonnegative in the example, t can only be 0 or 1.

**Reading and criticism:** [Algebra and Trigonometry 2e — Abramson and contributors](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/sources/Algebra-and-Trigonometry.lingua#r_7FKH7S2VNZT97STHJ10KB186YX) section 11.6 supplies augmented-matrix and elimination practice. Its opening square-system presentation should be read with the qualifications above: nonzero scaling, skipped pivots, and the distinction between echelon and reduced echelon forms. Work the source examples and one dependent rectangular case. Do not treat row reduction as preserving an unstated approximate-error objective; that question is addressed later in this block.

